---
import InsertLayout from '../../layouts/InsertLayout.astro';
import { breadcrumbSchema } from '../../utils/jsonld';

const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
    { name: 'Public-surface audit', url: '/audit' }
  ]),
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Service',
    name: 'Public-surface audit',
    serviceType: 'Technical audit',
    description:
      'A fixed-scope audit of everything a stranger can verify about a business from outside it, delivered with the script that reproduces every finding.',
    provider: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'Toledo Technologies LLC',
      url: 'https://toledotechnologies.com'
    },
    areaServed: 'Worldwide',
    offers: [
      {
        '@type': 'Offer',
        name: 'Public-surface audit',
        price: '400',
        priceCurrency: 'USD',
        url: 'https://toledotechnologies.com/audit'
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Offer',
        name: 'Re-check within 90 days',
        price: '150',
        priceCurrency: 'USD',
        url: 'https://toledotechnologies.com/audit'
      }
    ]
  }
];
---

<InsertLayout
  title="Public-surface audit — US$400"
  description="Fifty-two checks on everything a stranger can verify about your business from outside it: mail authentication, domain and certificate risk, HTTP behaviour, and whether your pages say the same thing to a machine that they say to a person. Delivered with the script that reproduces every finding."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  heading="Public-surface audit"
  subheading="US$400. Fifty-two checks run from outside, with the script that reproduces every one of them."
>
  <p>
    Everything about your business that a stranger can check without your
    permission is already checkable. Your mail authentication records, your
    domain registration, the certificates issued in your name, what your pages
    say to something that reads them mechanically rather than looking at them.
    Customers do not check any of it. Procurement reviewers, insurers, acquirers
    and people impersonating you all do.
  </p>
  <p>
    This audit is that check, written down. No access to anything you own. No
    credentials, no logins, no agent on your server, nothing to install. You
    give us a domain name and we give you back what is visible from outside it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/audit/toledo-technologies">
      The complete worked example is our own site, audited by the same script,
      with the fourteen findings it produced.</a
    >
  </p>

  <h2>What you get</h2>
  <p>
    By email, within two business days of you confirming the scope in writing:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>The report</strong>, readable and machine-readable. Every one of
      52 checks, each marked <em>pass</em>, <em>finding</em> or
      <em>not established</em>, with the evidence that produced it. Findings are ranked, and each one says what it
      actually costs you if it stays as it is — not a score, not a grade, not a
      letter.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The evidence.</strong> Every DNS answer, HTTP response, header and
      certificate we looked at, captured as bytes with a SHA-256 digest over the
      set. If you disagree with a conclusion, you can read what it was drawn
      from.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The script.</strong> One Python file, standard library only, no
      dependencies to install and no key to obtain. Run it against your own
      domain and it produces the same report. Run it against the saved evidence
      and it produces that report byte for byte, without touching the network.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The acceptance test</h2>
  <p>
    Stated before you buy, because a finding you cannot check is just an
    opinion with a price on it:
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong
      >Every finding in the report reproduces when you run the included script
      against your own domain.</strong
    > A finding that does not reproduce is withdrawn and the report is reissued
    without it. If more than three findings are withdrawn, the audit is free and
    you keep everything.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is a real risk to take, so it is worth saying how it is survivable: the
    script is what produces the findings in the first place. There is no second,
    softer, hand-written list. The report is a reading of the script's output,
    and the script is the thing you are given.
  </p>
  <p>
    It has already caught one. A draft of the checker reported that our own
    certificate did not cover <code>www</code>. It does — <code>www</code>
    presents its own certificate, which the check was not connecting to. The
    finding was withdrawn, the check was rewritten to connect to each hostname
    separately, and
    <a href="/audit/toledo-technologies#withdrawn"
      >the withdrawal is written into the worked example</a
    > rather than quietly deleted.
  </p>

  <h2>What the 52 checks cover</h2>

  <h3>Mail authentication and impersonation surface — 10 checks</h3>
  <p>
    SPF presence, uniqueness, terminal qualifier, and the DNS lookup budget —
    the 10-lookup limit that turns SPF into a permanent error once a vendor you
    added last year widens their own record, silently and with no symptom you
    would ever notice. DMARC presence, policy strength, sampling percentage and
    reporting address. DKIM selectors. MTA-STS and TLS reporting. MX.
  </p>
  <p>
    The question these answer is specific: can a stranger send an invoice that
    appears to come from your billing address and have your customer's mail
    provider deliver it to the inbox? For most small businesses the answer is
    yes, and the record that would stop it is one DNS line.
  </p>

  <h3>Domain and certificate continuity — 9 checks</h3>
  <p>
    Registration expiry and registrar transfer lock, read from RDAP. Nameserver
    count and concentration. DNSSEC. CAA. Certificate expiry, issuer, and
    whether every hostname you use presents a certificate that names it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Plus Certificate Transparency: the public log of every certificate ever
    issued for your domain. It routinely names subdomains the owner forgot
    existed — staging copies, a vendor's old integration host, a subdomain from
    a project that ended. They are public regardless of whether you linked them.
  </p>

  <h3>How the site behaves before a browser renders it — 9 checks</h3>
  <p>
    Plain HTTP redirecting to HTTPS. Whether the apex and <code>www</code> forms
    converge or both serve. HSTS and its max-age. The five standard protection
    headers, and frame protection. Cookies set on a plain GET, and their flags.
    Software versions disclosed in headers. Compression. Median response time
    over repeated samples from one location.
  </p>

  <h3>What your pages say to something that reads them — 13 checks</h3>
  <p>
    robots.txt and what it permits, sitemaps followed through their indexes, and
    whether the URLs they list actually answer. security.txt. llms.txt. Titles
    and descriptions, missing or duplicated. Canonical URLs. Link preview
    metadata. Structured data — present, parsing, and what it declares. Internal
    links that do not resolve. Plain-HTTP subresources on HTTPS pages.
  </p>

  <h3>Whether your claims agree with each other — 5 checks</h3>
  <p>
    This is the part no free tool does. The same fact is published in several
    places and they drift apart:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>prices in your visible text against prices in your structured data</li>
    <li>
      the contact address in your markup against the one in your structured data
    </li>
    <li>
      whether one legal entity name is used, or several variants of it
    </li>
    <li>
      whether anything reading the HTML can identify a next step for a buyer at
      all
    </li>
    <li>whether any policy page carries a date it took effect</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A price that appears one way to a reader and another way to a shopping
    surface is not a design problem. It is two different answers to the same
    question, published by you, and the machine-readable one is the one that
    gets quoted.
  </p>

  <h3>The machine-checkable part of accessibility — 6 checks</h3>
  <p>
    Images with no <code>alt</code> attribute and images with no declared
    dimensions. Documents with no <code>lang</code>. Heading outlines that skip
    or repeat levels. Link text that does not say where the link goes. Form
    fields with no programmatic label.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>This is a defect list, not a conformance statement.</strong> It is
    the subset a parser can settle from static HTML. Colour contrast, focus
    order, keyboard traps and anything requiring a rendered page are outside it,
    and we do not issue accessibility conformance statements of any kind.
  </p>

  <h2>What this deliberately does not do</h2>
  <p>
    The boundary matters more than the coverage, so it is enforced in the script
    rather than promised in prose:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>GET and HEAD only.</strong> No POST, no form submission, no login
      attempt, no credential of any kind.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No path guessing.</strong> It requests the domain you name, pages
      linked from those pages, URLs in your own sitemap, and a fixed list of
      conventional well-known files. It does not go looking for
      <code>.env</code>, <code>.git</code>, backups or admin panels — the files
      that only exist by mistake.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No vulnerability scanning, no fuzzing, no port scanning.</strong>
      Ports 80 and 443 on the hostnames you name, and nothing else.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rate limited and identified.</strong> One request at a time, with
      a User-Agent that says what it is and links here.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Everything past that line is a different kind of work, it needs written
    authorisation from whoever owns the system, and it is not what this is.
  </p>

  <h2>The script is free</h2>
  <p>
    Worth being direct about, because you will work it out in five minutes
    anyway.
    <a href="/audit/public_surface_check.py" download data-astro-reload
      >The checker is published under the MIT licence</a
    > and you can run it yourself, today, for nothing. It is one file and it needs
    nothing installed.
  </p>
  <p>What US$400 buys is the part the script cannot do:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Which of the findings matter for you.</strong> A script hands back
      fourteen items. Two of them can cost you money this quarter and eleven are
      hygiene. Deciding which is which needs to know what your business
      actually is, and that is a judgement, not a check.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The exact change, written out.</strong> Not "enable DMARC" — the
      record, the value, the order to apply it in, and what breaks if you skip a
      step.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What we could not establish and why.</strong> Checks that could
      not complete are reported as such, never as passes, and the report says
      what would settle them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Being wrong in writing.</strong> The reproduce-or-withdraw term
      above. A free script has no such obligation to you.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If reading the script's output yourself is enough, do that instead. That is
    a real outcome and we would rather you got it.
  </p>

  <h2>What it costs, and when you pay</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>US$400</strong> — one domain, up to 60 pages crawled, delivered
      within two business days of written scope confirmation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>US$150</strong> — a re-check of the same domain within 90 days,
      which reports what changed since the first run rather than repeating it.
      The point of this one is to prove the fixes landed.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>You pay after the files arrive, not before</strong> — the same term
    as every other engagement here. We confirm the scope and this acceptance
    test in writing, deliver, and invoice after you have the report and the
    evidence in your hands. Your risk on a first engagement is your own reading
    time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Prices are in US dollars and exclude any tax applicable in your
    jurisdiction. Additional domains are US$250 each when run together.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two business days is the term, not a best case. This is a one-operator
    supplier; if a run cannot be delivered in that window, you will be told
    before work starts rather than after, and you can walk away at no cost.
  </p>

  <h2>What would make this a bad fit</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>If you want a certificate or an attestation.</strong> This is a
      defect list with evidence. It is not a penetration test, a SOC 2 artifact,
      a WCAG conformance statement, or anything a regulator or an insurer will
      accept as an assessment. If someone has told you that you need one of
      those, buy that instead.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>If the problem is inside the system.</strong> Everything here is
      visible from outside. Application logic, your database, your authenticated
      pages and anything behind a login are all invisible to it, and a clean
      report says nothing about them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>If you need someone on a call.</strong> The work is asynchronous
      and delivered in writing. There are no calls.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>If your site is one page with no mail on the domain.</strong> Half
      the checks will report nothing to report, and you will have paid US$400
      for the half that were worth running. Say so and we will tell you whether
      it is worth it before you pay.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Limits of the method, stated plainly</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Timing is measured from one location with no browser. It is connection and
      server time, not page load, and it is not a Core Web Vitals measurement.
    </li>
    <li>
      DKIM selectors are arbitrary strings. Conventional names are probed; a
      selector found is proof of signing, but nothing found is not proof of
      absence, and it is reported as not established rather than as a failure.
    </li>
    <li>
      The HTML parser does not execute JavaScript. A page that assembles itself
      in the browser is largely invisible to it, and the report says so rather
      than reporting an empty page as a finding.
    </li>
    <li>
      Form-label detection does not see a wrapping <code>&lt;label&gt;</code>.
      That check produces a list to verify, and is labelled as such.
    </li>
    <li>
      Third-party services — crt.sh, RDAP, public DNS resolvers — are sometimes
      unavailable. When they are, the affected check is reported as not
      established, with the reason. A check that could not run is never counted
      as a pass.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Starting one</h2>
  <p>
    Email <a href="mailto:dev@toledotechnologies.com">dev@toledotechnologies.com</a>
    with the domain. That is the whole intake — there is nothing to fill in and
    nothing to grant us.
  </p>
  <p>
    You will get a written confirmation repeating the scope, the price and this
    acceptance test before any work starts, and the files within two business
    days of your reply agreeing to it. If the answer is that your public surface
    is in good shape, you get a report that says so, with the evidence, and that
    is a legitimate result rather than a reason to manufacture findings.
  </p>
  <p>
    Toledo Technologies LLC is the contracting supplier, and it is AI-operated:
    correspondence, the audit itself and the written report are run by AI, with
    the company accountable for delivery. How that works is set out on the
    <a href="/about">about page</a>. Other fixed-price work is at
    <a href="/engagements">/engagements</a>, and the runnable code samples behind
    the method are at <a href="/proofs">/proofs</a>.
  </p>
</InsertLayout>
